\ cv8.4 - make the Forth compiler emit CV8 instead of cells.
\
\ Phase 3. Until now every CV8 image has been TRANSLATED from a cell
\ image by tools/layout.py, so a CV8 system could run but could not
\ compile a new definition.
\ This file replaces the code-emitting words with CV8 emitters.
\
\ HOW IT IS USED. Every word here has a distinct name (`;8`, `IF8`, ...)
\ because redefining `;` in the CELL image would break the cell
\ compiler on the very next definition. Load this, dump, and translate
\ with --cv8-compiler, which swaps each `X8` body into `X`. The overlay's own bodies are compiled by the cell
\ compiler and translated like any other word; what changes is that the
\ RESULTING image's compiler emits bytes. The cell image keeps its own
\ compiler untouched, so relf/relf32 are unaffected.
\
\ WHAT IS NOT TOUCHED. `,` and `C,` still append data - VARIABLE, ALLOT
\ and the dictionary header all rely on them. Only the words that
\ append CODE are redefined.
\
\ See CV8-REFERENCE.md for the encoding this emits.

\ ---- opcode numbers (CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.2) --------------------------
\ The primitive band is kernel.4's PRIMITIVE order, so these are fixed
\ small integers rather than the cell image's code-field addresses.
  1 CONSTANT EXIT-OP
  2 CONSTANT LIT16-OP
  3 CONSTANT BRANCH-OP
  4 CONSTANT 0BRANCH-OP
\ The synthetic opcodes sit immediately above kernel.4's primitives, so
\ every one of them moves when a primitive is added. They were written
\ out as 68..71 and 124 - correct for exactly as long as the count
\ stayed 68 - and a 69th primitive left this file emitting an opcode
\ one below the one the engine decodes, with nothing to warn either
\ side. The symptom was a call into uncompiled memory during the very
\ first cross-compile.
\
\ Derived from one number now. Each of these is a real word with a
\ real header, about 26 bytes in a CV8 image, so they are kept to the
\ ones actually used - an unused LIT8X-OP and a redundant FOLD-BASE0
\ cost 52 bytes in every image before they were noticed. sod16.py, gen-fold.py, gen-tos.py and
\ layout.py derive the same values from len(prims); this file cannot
\ read kernel.4, so NPRIM-OP is the one place that has to be kept in
\ step with it. The check below catches the failure that actually
\ happens when it drifts upward - the folded band running into the
\ specialised one - but nothing here can catch a NPRIM-OP that is
\ simply wrong, so it is worth a grep when a primitive is added.
 67 CONSTANT NPRIM-OP           \ kernel.4's PRIMITIVE count
NPRIM-OP     CONSTANT LIT32-OP
NPRIM-OP 1 + CONSTANT DOVAR-OP
NPRIM-OP 2 + CONSTANT DODOES-OP
NPRIM-OP 3 + CONSTANT LIT8-OP
NPRIM-OP 4 + CONSTANT LIT8X-OP          \ LIT8 then EXIT, folded
\ The escape and wide-literal opcodes sit at the top of the byte, above
\ the specialised band, and do NOT move with the primitive count.
125 CONSTANT LIT64-OP           \ 0x7D
126 CONSTANT ESC-OP             \ 0x7E
\ The specialised band (CV8-REFERENCE.md 7) is fixed at 0x61 too. Only
\ the opcodes this compiler emits are named; the locals and tiny-word
\ opcodes are emitted by other means.
 97 CONSTANT LIT0-OP            \ 0x61  push 0; 0x62 push 1; 0x63 push -1
100 CONSTANT VAR@-OP            \ 0x64  slot operand
101 CONSTANT VAR!-OP            \ 0x65
121 CONSTANT ADDI-OP            \ 0x79  signed byte operand; +1 is ;EXIT
123 CONSTANT EQI-OP             \ 0x7B  signed byte operand; +1 is ;EXIT

\ ---- raw emission ---------------------------------------------------
: OP, ( n --- )
\ Append one opcode byte.
  C, ;

: W, ( n --- )
\ Append a 16-bit little-endian operand.
  DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, ;

: 32, ( n --- )
\ Append a 32-bit little-endian operand.
  DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, ;

VARIABLE CV8-SHIFT-V ( --- a-addr )
\ The call/slot scale. 3 on a 64-bit cell, 2 on a 32-bit cell - or 0 in
\ the byte-granular-header layout, where cv8b.4 sets it. A VARIABLE and
\ not a constant so a second overlay can change it after this one is
\ loaded; everything below reads it through CV8-SHIFT.
: CV8-SHIFT-DEFAULT ( --- n ) 1 CELLS 8 = IF 3 ELSE 2 THEN ;
CV8-SHIFT-DEFAULT CV8-SHIFT-V !
: CV8-SHIFT ( --- n ) CV8-SHIFT-V @ ;

VARIABLE CV8-DOES-FAR? ( --- a-addr )
VARIABLE CV8-DOES-RESERVE ( --- a-addr )
\ How DOES> writes its call, and how much room CREATE leaves for it.
\ Two bytes by default; cv8b.4 sets three, because at a call scale of 0
\ the near form cannot reach past 16 KB.
0 CV8-DOES-FAR? ! 2 CV8-DOES-RESERVE !

VARIABLE LAST-OP ( --- a-addr )
\ Address of the primitive opcode just emitted, or 0 if the last thing
\ emitted cannot be folded.

\ ---- peephole state --------------------------------------------------
\ The translator rewrites four two-operation patterns into one opcode
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 7): `LIT n +` and `LIT n =` into ADDI/EQI, and
\ `var @` and `var !` into VAR@/VAR!. Compiled at run time they were
\ left as written, which made a natively compiled shell 2-2.8x slower
\ than a translated one (Iteration 243). So the compiler remembers
\ where the last literal and the last call started, and the second
\ operation of a pattern rewinds HERE over the first and emits the
\ combined opcode instead.
\
\ The one rule that makes this safe: NOTHING MAY BRANCH BETWEEN THE TWO.
\ Every word that creates a branch target at HERE - BEGIN, THEN, the
\ resolution of WHILE - calls NO-PEEP, and so does anything that emits
\ code which is not itself one of the two halves.
VARIABLE LAST-LIT ( --- a-addr )     \ where the last literal starts, or 0
VARIABLE LAST-LITV ( --- a-addr )    \ and its value
VARIABLE LAST-CALL ( --- a-addr )    \ where the last call starts, or 0
VARIABLE LAST-CALLXT ( --- a-addr )  \ and its target

: NO-PEEP ( --- )
\ Forget everything: the next operation starts a fresh pattern.
  0 LAST-OP ! 0 LAST-LIT ! 0 LAST-CALL ! ;

: REWIND ( a-addr --- )
\ Discard everything compiled from a-addr to HERE.
  HERE - ALLOT ;

: SLOT, ( a-addr --- )
\ Append a slot operand naming a-addr: a scaled offset like a call,
\ two bytes big-endian if it fits 15 bits, else three with the top bit
\ set (VARSLOT, CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.1).
  START @ - CV8-SHIFT RSHIFT
  DUP 32768 < IF
    DUP 8 RSHIFT C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE
    DUP 16 RSHIFT 128 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C,
  THEN ;

\ ---- literals -------------------------------------------------------
: LIT, ( n --- )
\ Append a literal in the narrowest form that holds it. U< does the
\ range tests, so a negative value falls through to the wide forms.
\ 0, 1 and -1 have one-byte opcodes. Every literal is remembered for
\ the ADDI/EQI peephole, and the LIT8 and LIT16 forms for folding
\ into `;` - both have a folded LIT;EXIT form.
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-LIT ! DUP LAST-LITV !
  DUP 0 = IF DROP LIT0-OP OP, EXIT THEN
  DUP 1 = IF DROP LIT0-OP 1 + OP, EXIT THEN
  DUP -1 = IF DROP LIT0-OP 2 + OP, EXIT THEN
  DUP 256 U<   IF HERE LAST-OP ! LIT8-OP  OP, C, EXIT THEN
  DUP 65536 U< IF HERE LAST-OP ! LIT16-OP OP, W, EXIT THEN
  1 CELLS 4 = IF LIT32-OP OP, 32, EXIT THEN
  \ 64-bit cell: does it fit a SIGNED 32-bit field? n + 2^31 < 2^32.
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< IF LIT32-OP OP, 32, EXIT THEN
  \ Wider than 32 bits needs a full cell. Before LIT64 existed such a
  \ value was silently truncated (CV8-REFERENCE.md 10).
  LIT64-OP OP, DUP 32, 32 RSHIFT 32, ;

: LITERAL8 ( n --- )
\ Add a literal to the current definition.
  LIT, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- folding prim;EXIT at runtime ------------------------------------
\ The translator folds a primitive followed by EXIT into one opcode
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 6.4), worth ~20%. Without this, code compiled at
\ RUN time misses that; with it, newly compiled code matches translated
\ code. Two things make it safe:
\   * the fold applies only to the LAST op of a definition, at `;`, so
\     nothing can branch past it into the EXIT;
\   * LAST-OP is cleared by anything that is not a plain primitive emit,
\     including every branch resolution - a THEN that resolves to HERE
\     would otherwise be left pointing at an EXIT that is no longer
\     there.

: >OP ( xt --- n )
\ The CV8 opcode of a PRIMITIVE, computed in the CELL image where this
\ file is compiled: a cell-image primitive token is 1 + n * CELL, and n
\ is exactly the CV8 opcode (both are the position in kernel.4).
  @ 1- 1 CELLS / ;

CREATE FOLD-OPS ( --- a-addr )
\ The foldable primitives as OPCODE BYTES, in the SAME ORDER as the
\ translator's --fold-set and the engine's generated table. Bytes, not
\ xts: an xt is an absolute address of the cell session and would not
\ survive translation - the first version of this stored xts and read
\ back garbage.
  ' + >OP C,  ' = >OP C,  ' ! >OP C,  ' @ >OP C,
  ' LSHIFT >OP C,  ' RSHIFT >OP C,  ' C@ >OP C,  ' C! >OP C,
  ' AND >OP C,  ' OR >OP C,  ' XOR >OP C,  ' LIT >OP C,
  ' < >OP C,  ' U< >OP C,  ' OVER >OP C,  ' DROP >OP C,
  ' DUP >OP C,  ' SWAP >OP C,  ' ROT >OP C,  ' >R >OP C,
  ' R> >OP C,  ' R@ >OP C,  ' NEGATE >OP C,
ALIGN
23 CONSTANT #FOLD-OPS
\ The four primitives the peepholes complete, as opcodes for the same
\ reason FOLD-OPS holds opcodes and not xts.
' + >OP CONSTANT PLUS-OP
' = >OP CONSTANT EQUALS-OP
' @ >OP CONSTANT FETCH-OP
' ! >OP CONSTANT STORE-OP
NPRIM-OP 5 + CONSTANT FOLD-BASE
\ The folded band starts just above the synthetic opcodes and grows up
\ towards the specialised band at 0x61. They collided the first time a
\ 69th primitive was added: the last folded opcode and `lit0` became
\ the same byte, and the engine decoded one as the other.
FOLD-BASE #FOLD-OPS + 97 >
ABORT" cv8.4: the folded band has reached the specialised band - too many primitives"

: FOLD-OP ( op --- op2 -1 | op 0 )
\ The folded opcode for op, if it has one: a primitive from the table,
\ LIT8, or one of the two immediates, whose ;EXIT form is the next
\ opcode up.
  DUP LIT8-OP = IF DROP LIT8X-OP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP ADDI-OP = OVER EQI-OP = OR IF 1 + -1 EXIT THEN
  #FOLD-OPS 0 DO
    DUP FOLD-OPS I + C@ = IF
      DROP I FOLD-BASE + -1 UNLOOP EXIT
    THEN
  LOOP 0 ;

\ ---- calls ----------------------------------------------------------
: CALL, ( a-addr --- )
\ Append a call to the body at a-addr: a scaled offset from the image
\ base, near (2 bytes) if it fits in 14 bits, far (3 bytes) otherwise.
\ Remembered for the VAR@/VAR! peephole.
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-CALL ! DUP LAST-CALLXT !
  START @ - CV8-SHIFT RSHIFT
  DUP 16384 < IF
    DUP 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE
    DUP 16 RSHIFT 192 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C,
  THEN ;

: IMM-PAIR? ( op1 op2 --- f )
\ Is op1 the opcode of + (op2 = ADDI) or = (op2 = EQI), following a
\ literal that fits a signed byte? The patterns are `LIT n +` and
\ `LIT n =`; nothing else has an immediate form.
  ADDI-OP = IF PLUS-OP ELSE EQUALS-OP THEN =
  LAST-LIT @ 0 = 0 = AND
  LAST-LITV @ 128 + 256 U< AND ;

: PEEP-IMM ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `LIT n +` or `LIT n =` just compiled, if that is what op
\ completes. The result can itself fold into `;`.
  DUP ADDI-OP IMM-PAIR? IF DROP ADDI-OP ELSE
  DUP EQI-OP IMM-PAIR? IF DROP EQI-OP ELSE DROP 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-LIT @ REWIND LAST-LITV @ >R NO-PEEP
  HERE LAST-OP ! OP, R> 255 AND C, -1 ;

: PEEP-VAR ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `var @` or `var !` just compiled, where var is a plain data
\ word - its body starts with DOVAR, so its parameter field is fixed.
  LAST-CALL @ 0 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-CALLXT @ C@ DOVAR-OP = 0 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP FETCH-OP = IF DROP VAR@-OP ELSE
  DUP STORE-OP = IF DROP VAR!-OP ELSE DROP 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-CALL @ REWIND LAST-CALLXT @ >R NO-PEEP
  OP, R> >BODY SLOT, -1 ;

: COMPILE,8 ( xt --- )
\ Add the execution semantics of xt to the current definition: a
\ primitive becomes its opcode byte, anything else a call.
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND IF
    \ A primitive's CV8 body is one opcode byte - C@, not @: the cell
    \ compiler read a whole cell here because a token WAS a cell.
    \ An escaped primitive's body is [ESC][selector]; inline both bytes
    \ and do not offer it for folding.
    DUP C@ ESC-OP = IF
      NO-PEEP DUP C@ OP, 1+ C@ OP,
    ELSE
      C@ DUP PEEP-IMM IF DROP EXIT THEN
      DUP PEEP-VAR IF DROP EXIT THEN
      NO-PEEP HERE LAST-OP ! OP,
    THEN
  ELSE
    CALL,
  THEN ;

: EXIT, ( --- )
\ Append a return, folded into the last operation where it has a
\ folded form. A final 0 or 1 is re-emitted as LIT8 so that it, too,
\ folds. Only ever used at the END of a body, so nothing can branch to
\ the EXIT that folding removes.
  LAST-LIT @ IF LAST-LITV @ 2 U< IF
    LAST-LIT @ REWIND LAST-LITV @ LIT8-OP LAST-LIT @ LAST-OP ! OP, C,
  THEN THEN
  LAST-OP @ DUP IF
    DUP C@ FOLD-OP IF SWAP C! ELSE 2DROP EXIT-OP OP, THEN
  ELSE DROP EXIT-OP OP, THEN
  NO-PEEP ;

: ;8 ( --- )
\ Finish the current definition.
  EXIT, 0 STATE ! ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- control flow ---------------------------------------------------
\ A branch operand is a signed 16-bit BYTE offset measured from the
\ operand itself (CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.3) - not from past it, and not in
\ cells. >MARK leaves the operand's address for >RESOLVE to patch.

: >MARK ( --- a-addr )
\ Emit a placeholder branch operand and leave its address.
  NO-PEEP HERE 0 W, ;

: !W ( n a-addr --- )
\ Store a 16-bit little-endian value.
  2DUP C! 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP C! ;

: >RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
\ Patch the operand at a-addr so its branch lands on HERE. Clears
\ LAST-OP: something now branches HERE, so the op before it must not
\ be folded away.
  NO-PEEP DUP HERE SWAP - SWAP !W ;

: <RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
\ Emit a branch operand jumping back to a-addr.
  NO-PEEP HERE - W, ;

: BEGIN8 ( --- a-addr )
\ HERE becomes a branch target, so no pattern may span it.
  NO-PEEP HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL8 ( a-addr --- )
  NO-PEEP 0BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: AGAIN8 ( a-addr --- )
  NO-PEEP BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: IF8 ( --- a-addr )
  NO-PEEP 0BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN8 ( a-addr --- )
  >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE8 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 )
  NO-PEEP BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK SWAP >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE8 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 a-addr1 )
  POSTPONE IF8 SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT8 ( a-addr1 a-addr2 --- )
  \ ( a-addr1 a-addr2 --- ) with the BEGIN address on top, as WHILE8
  \ left it: <RESOLVE takes that one, >RESOLVE patches WHILE's mark.
  NO-PEEP BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- defining words -------------------------------------------------
: CREATE8 ( "ccc" --- )
\ A data body is [DOVAR][pad to a cell][parameter field], so the
\ parameter field stays cell-aligned and the pad is never executed.
  \ Two bytes reserved after DOVAR so that DOES> can overwrite the front
  \ with [DODOES][2-byte call] without moving the parameter field. The
  \ engine computes the PFA as align(body+3) on both paths.
  HEADER REVEAL NO-PEEP DOVAR-OP OP, CV8-DOES-RESERVE @ ALLOT ALIGN ;

: VARIABLE8 ( "ccc" --- )
  CREATE8 0 , ;

: CONSTANT8 ( x "ccc" --- )
  HEADER REVEAL LIT, EXIT, ;

: RECURSE8 ( --- )
  LAST @ NAME> CALL, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DO ... LOOP ----------------------------------------------------
\ (LOOP) and (+LOOP) read an inline CELL operand, and it must stay
\ cell-aligned - the one place CV8 keeps cell granularity
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.3). The translator pads BEFORE the call so the
\ operand lands aligned just after it; the runtime compiler does the
\ same. The pad is NOOPs, which do execute, once per loop.
: OPERAND-ALIGN ( --- )
\ Emit NOOPs until a 2-byte call would leave HERE cell-aligned.
  BEGIN HERE 2 + 1 CELLS 1- AND WHILE 0 C, REPEAT ;

: DO8 ( --- a-addr )
\ (DO) has no inline operand, so this matches the cell version.
  POSTPONE (DO) NO-PEEP 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LOOP8 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP
  ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP8 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (+LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP
  ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- ?DO and LEAVE ---------------------------------------------------
\ (?DO) and (LEAVE) also carry an inline CELL operand, so they need the
\ same alignment as (LOOP).
: ?DO8 ( --- a-addr )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (?DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 'LEAVE ! 0 , NO-PEEP HERE
  ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE8 ( --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LEAVE) HERE 'LEAVE @ , 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DOES> -----------------------------------------------------------
\ A DOES> word's body is [DODOES][2-byte call to the tail][pad][PFA],
\ where the cell version wrote a single relative-call cell. CREATE8 has
\ already laid down [DOVAR][pad], so there is room to overwrite.

: !CALL ( a-addr target --- )
\ Store a call to target at a-addr, in the form CV8-DOES-FAR? selects.
\
\ The two-byte near form has a 14-bit field of SCALED units: 131 KB of
\ reach at a scale of 3, but only 16 KB at a scale of 0. Cross-compiling
\ the kernel grows the dictionary well past 16 KB, so at scale 0 a DOES>
\ word up there had its target silently truncated and jumped into the
\ data stack. The far form is three bytes, which is why CREATE has to
\ reserve a matching amount - see CV8-DOES-RESERVE.
  START @ - CV8-SHIFT RSHIFT        ( a-addr v )
  CV8-DOES-FAR? @ IF
    2DUP 255 AND SWAP 2 + C!
    2DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!
    16 RSHIFT 192 OR SWAP C!
  ELSE
    2DUP 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!
    8 RSHIFT 128 OR SWAP C!
  THEN ;

: (;CODE)8 ( --- )
\ Runtime for DOES>: point the last created word at the code following
\ the call to this word, and end the defining word (the R> discards its
\ return address, exactly as the cell version does).
  \ ALIGNED because DOES>8 pads to the next call-target boundary: the
  \ return address points at the pad, the tail starts after it.
  R> ALIGNED LAST @ NAME>
  DODOES-OP OVER C!
  1+ SWAP !CALL ;

: DOES>8 ( --- )
  \ The tail must be an ALIGNED call target: a call is a SCALED offset,
  \ so an unaligned tail address loses its low bits and jumps to the
  \ wrong place. The pad is dead code - (;CODE) never returns here.
  POSTPONE (;CODE) ALIGN NO-PEEP POSTPONE R> ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- debugging aid --------------------------------------------------
: BYTES. ( a-addr n --- )
\ Print n bytes from a-addr. Compiled by the CELL compiler and
\ translated, so it works even when the runtime compiler is broken.
  0 DO DUP I + C@ . LOOP DROP CR ;
